 "reading": "What Dolby's decoders have more in the coupled region of an E-AC-3 stream is a decorrelation. For a coupled coefficient X they write a X + b D(X) with a^2 + b^2 = 1, D an all-pass of three poles along the blocks after a delay of one block (a real pole at 0,7555, a pair at radius 0,7237 and 117,18 degrees), the pair another for every block and coupling sub-band and the second channel's b of the other sign. On four test streams the fitted taps are that all-pass to 1.1e-05 and the model gives Dolby's coefficients to 89.4 to 95.0 dB. It is set off by what the channels have of their own under the coupling begin frequency: streams without it stand at 92.0 to 113.8 dB in the coupled region as E-AC-3 and as AC-3, streams with it at 0.1 to 6.3 dB as E-AC-3 and at 89.3 to 105.9 as AC-3. Both of Dolby's decoders here do the same, to 109.7 dB of each other. The objects of a JOC stream are made of the plain core. The rule for the pair is not read, so this decoder leaves the coupled region as the standard has it, and the conformance matrix says not implemented where it said unknown."
